Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>People are resistance to AI largely because AI is moralized and presented as something that is morally reprehensible, even though it would have concrete benefits for people.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This could explain why there is so much public resistant to AI despite AI having proven benefits for people.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The conclusion in this note was drawn based:</p><ul><li>Analysis of new sources covering AI, which showed a similar level of moralization as other controversial topics like GMOs and vaccines.</li><li>Surveys which show that people would not use AI even if it produced concrete benefits for them.</li><li>On when people were given tasks to do, they would not use AI, even when it would have aided them.</li></ul><p>There are several reasons why this resistance has developed:</p><ul><li><strong>The Naturalistic Fallacy:</strong> The belief that \"natural\" things are inherently good, and \"artificial\" things are inherently bad.</li><li><strong>Sacred Domain Intrusion:</strong> The fact that AI is intruding on domains that have historically been dominated by humans, such as artistic creativity, romantic companionship, and legal judgement.</li><li><strong>Social Contagion:</strong> Because others also do not like AI, having the same opinion allows the individual to fit into a larger group, overriding individual pragmatism and reality.</li></ul><p>The way to deal with this viewing of AI through a strictly moral lens (which even overrides the utility benefits that AI has), is to explicate the benefits of AI equally in moral terms.</p><ul><li>Ex. AI helps reduce the amount of medical mistakes that could occur, saving more lives.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The reasons for the decline of Buddhism in India includes the lack of patronage in India, and the fact that Buddhism was overtaken by the <em>Bhakti</em> movement (a spawn of the incumbent Hindu religion), a movement with roots in Hinduism, around the turn of the first milenium.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Many of the elements of Buddhism were eventually incorporated into Hinduism.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Bhakti Hindu movement overtook Buddhism in India around 1000AD\">The Bhakti Hindu movement overtook Buddhism in India around 1000AD</a><ul><li>The second cause named above, with its dates and the mechanism that made Hindu practice accessible.</li></ul></li></ul>","Great art carries meaning that cannot be paraphrased":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A work has depth when its content cannot be extracted and restated without loss, which typically shows up as the availability of two readings that the work sustains at once.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the criterion that distinguishes art from illustration, since an illustrated thesis is by construction paraphrasable.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The test case is a work that supports a reading against its own apparent intention. <em>Parasite</em> is ostensibly about structural inequality yet goes out of its way to make the poor unsympathetic, and it is good partly because it sustains both readings. Plato’s <em>Apology</em> can be read as a condemnation of Socrates rather than a defence.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together\">Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together</a><ul><li>The structural feature that makes multiple readings possible.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Recognizing one’s failures late does not by itself constitute redemption\">Recognizing one’s failures late does not by itself constitute redemption</a><ul><li>An instance of a work sustaining two readings of its own ending.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy\">Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy</a><ul><li>Another case where the surface reading and the available reading diverge.</li></ul></li></ul>","In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Under a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not undertaken merely as a means to satisfy some need. It is also (largely) taken as an end in and of itself.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of Weber’s argument in The Protestant Work Ethic.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>I think that while this is true to a certain extent, “in and of itself” is too overarching of a statement to me. More accurately, it would be that capitalism incentives economic acquisition as a means of providing more economic acquisition in the future (the accumulated wealth being thought of as economic capital). In a sense, Weber is right that the accumulation of capital can be thought of as being in and of itself (since capital is useless on its own), though this explanation makes it more clear.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Protestant Work Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism\">The Protestant Work Ethic</a></li></ul>","In signal transduction pathways, enzymes cause signal amplification":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In signal transcription pathways, enzymes are the locations where the signal is amplified.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant for AP Bio, especially understanding how signal pathways work.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In RTKs, signals are amplified through kinase cascades; in GPCR, signals are amplified through second messengers\">In RTKs, signals are amplified through kinase cascades; in GPCR, signals are amplified through second messengers</a><ul><li>The two receptor families in which this amplification is realized differently.</li></ul></li></ul>","Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The “Five Relationships” that Confucianism followed are examples of religiously entrenched power imbalances designed to “preserve harmony.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This could explain the vast disparities in power that exist in modern China, by tracing the normalization of such power disparities to a keystone in Chinese culture.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Society consists primarily of unequal relationship; i.e., father and son, husband and wife, ruler and subject. At the same time, positions of power could be achieved meritocracy.<ul><li>This provided justification for inequality in society, but also set the expectation that those in power would yield the power justly and care for those below them.</li></ul></li><li>By maintaining these relationships, harmony would prevail.</li></ul><p>In this way, Confucian ethics can be said to emphasize gradations, where some (such as one’s parents) are loved more than others (like a stranger). This is in contrast with Mohism’s teachings.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives\">Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives</a><ul><li>It is these categorical imperatives that maintain the rigid social order.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions\">The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions</a><ul><li>This is perhaps the reason for the strictly hierarchical Chinese state.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns, such as coordination, efficiency, stability, etc.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the importance of the idea of justice in Rawls' philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Rawls</p><p>\"laws and institutions no matter how efficient and well-arranged must be reformed or abolished if they are unjust.\"</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4>","Law is not constitutive of justice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The laws of some state does not constitute justice. In other words, it is not the case that whatever the laws say is exactly what justice is.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This fundamentally weakens the justificatory power of law and the nature of the legal system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is proven by the fact that there are and have been obviously unjust laws, and therefore, laws could not possibly be the only valid interpretation of justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law</a><ul><li>It seems the Aristotle here is making a claim opposed to this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Countries such as China, Japan, and India are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist in that they adopt capitalist methods of production, but many aspects of the economy are controlled by the state or are directly influenced by the state.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This adds nuance to the usual characterization of East Asian countries as either capitalist or socialist, or some other vague term.</p><h4>Examples</h4><h5>Japan</h5><ul><li>Business cooperated deeply with the government:<ul><li>Many of the biggest international Japanese companies were subsidized by the government.<ul><li>Mainly automobile, technology/manufacturing, and steel.</li></ul></li><li>Many were government monopolies, though encouraged (through low tariffs) by the government to trade internationally.</li></ul></li><li>High savings rates in the country:<ul><li>40% of savings rate; promoted low interest rates and growth of infrastructure.</li><li>Foreign savings depreciated the yen as well.<ul><li>Promoted the success of Japanese businesses.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Deep investments in education (human capital):<ul><li>Made the country technologically sophisticated.</li></ul></li><li>Great political stability.<ul><li>Predictable future means that investment is encouraged (perceived lower risk).</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Neo-mercantilism is the modern adaptation of historical mercantilism\">Neo-mercantilism is the modern adaptation of historical mercantilism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized\">The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence\">The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li></ul>","One should act differently depending on one's relative size compared to the overall system":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When one is a small and inconsequential actor in a system, one should act from generally applicable and utility generating principles (rule utilitarianism). Only when one becomes such a major actor in a system that one’s actions generate profound and reverberating effects in an system that one should act with consideration of the other actors in the system (act utilitarianism).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a simple heuristic that helps with understanding the way that we should act in different situations.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When you are such a negligible part of the overall system, the best way of acting is a non-consequentialist one, i.e., acting in a way that is in accordance only with personal utility, and not overall utility of the system. This is because one’s actions at this stage is unlikely to have large effects that perpetuate in the system very far and thus adversely affect oneself now or in the future (there, of course, are exceptions to this, via coordination problems/races to the bottom). In cases where one is in fact in a influential position in a system, one should then begin to consider one’s consequences in the system, since it will not begin to directly and majorly affect oneself.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems analysis works best when the system is static\">Systems analysis works best when the system is static</a><ul><li>The issue perhaps with this heuristic is that since systems are constantly changing, it is difficult to get a precise estimate of the size of the system. Although it is rare for there to be systems that fluctuate extremely violently in size.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a><ul><li>Perhaps one of the factors that lead to this equilibrium is this heuristic in action?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained\">Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained</a><ul><li>Decentralized systems are systems whereby individuals are able to act with personal discretion and according to their own individual heuristics.</li></ul></li></ul>","Plate 16 from 'The Disasters of War', or 'They make use of them'":"<h4>Addendum</h4><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Plate 9 from &#x27;The Disasters of War&#x27;, or &#x27;They don&#x27;t like to&#x27;\">Plate 9 from 'The Disasters of War', or 'They don't like to'</a><ul><li>From the same series.</li></ul></li></ul>","Property should be privately held, but commonly used":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Property should be privately held, since doing so establishes particular virtues that would otherwise simply be missing. However, people should continue to act with the virtue of liberality and allow their property to be used by others most of the time.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>While Aristotle completely rejects the idea that property should be coercively commonly distributed, he does believe that people should share their property with others under most circumstances, out of their own virtue.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property allows for the preservation of specific virtues\">Private property allows for the preservation of specific virtues</a><ul><li>This is the reason that property is kept private.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy\">Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy</a><ul><li>Modern economics backs up Aristotle’s proposition that property should be held privately.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property facilitates trade\">Private property facilitates trade</a><ul><li>It seems that Aristotle’s solution would prevent trade from occurring in the traditional sense, since people could just borrow from others.</li></ul></li></ul>","Rentier states typically have high inequality and low political participation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rentier states, which are states that rely primarily on income from natural resources, tend to have high inequality and low political participation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains crucial properties that rentier states typically have, which allow us to analyze such states with the appropriate context.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Since the resource wealth of natural resources tend to be concentrated (there are high fixed costs to extracting natural resources), the wealth tends to flow towards only the upper class.</li><li>Since there is a lot of wealth coming through with the natural resources, the state does not have a reason to raise taxes. This decreases the incentives for people to participate politically, since taxation is typically a very politically salient issue.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics\">Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics</a><ul><li>A rentier state would have a political culture that does not stress political engagement, since people don’t really have an incentive to do so.</li></ul></li></ul>","Sea power translates into national power":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>At core of Mahanian naval strategy is that naval power translates into national power, i.e., who controls the seas is able to exert heavy influence on other nations. This dictates, then that nations strive to lock the opposing side in their ports, without allowing them to have any chance at control of the sea, which then naturally leads to the idea of “decisive battle” to decide control of the sea.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Mahanian naval warfare influenced many people, including Teddy Roosevelt, Wilhelm II, and the Japanese military. The core of theory originates with the idea of naval control being the prerequisite to national power.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Naval power is the primary determinant for national power as control of the seas allows for isolation of the enemy from their allies, from supplies, and from strategic maneuvers involving the sea. This idea of the preeminence of naval strategy naturally leads to Mahan’s concept of “decisive battle,” where through a single decisive battle, control of the sea, and therefore and in large, the nature of the war, would be decided.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world\">Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a></li></ul>","Secular philosophies of history can only address humans as collectives, and not individuals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the failures of the many secular philosophies of history that have existed is that they fail to address the individual specifically; rather, they are often just addressed towards “humanity” in general.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the critiques the Lowith levels at the secular philosophies of history (Marx, Comte, etc.).</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Specifically, I think that this critique is relevant when comparing these other secular philosophies of history with Christianity, since salvation in Christianity refers uniquely to the individual soul. Other secular philosophies of history, such as the positivist philosophy of Comte or the economic materialist philosophy of Marx, must take as their subject either \"humanity\" or the \"Occident\" in the case of the former, and the \"proletariat\" or the \"bourgeoisie\" in the case of the latter. But none of these exists except in individual men and women, and so none of them can be the bearer of a destiny. These philosophies therefore adopt the universal element of the Christian understanding of history while eliminating the concern with persons that alone made that universality intelligible.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity\">Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity</a><ul><li>This is referenced in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The final stage of history is a positive one\">The final stage of history is a positive one</a><ul><li>This idea of Comte is also referenced in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Struggles during youth are meaningful":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that our struggles during our youth have some special meaning, something which disappears during our struggle in our mid and late life.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an oft-referenced idea in literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>In <em>Youth</em>, Marlow’s struggles during his youth are said by himself to have been meaningful, perhaps both for their aesthetic value and also the practical experience gained. </li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Suffering has metaphysical meaning":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The general idea that there is some meaning to the suffering that humans have to endure, either because suffering itself is intrinsically metaphysically valuable or because it is instrumentally valuable to help us develop certain virtuous traits.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant idea within many schools of philosophy, including as a major part of theodicies.</p><h4>Examples</h4><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering is good, if done correctly\">Suffering is good, if done correctly</a> -</li></ul>","Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle argues that to be truly happy, one must not only be virtuous (embody “goodness”) but must also have proper material conditions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This portrays Aristotle’s philosophy as one that is eminently practical and realistic, rather than idealistic and aesthetic.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation\">Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a></li></ul>","The Brothers Karamazov":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A sweeping philosophical novel that follows the turbulent lives of three brothers, Dmitri, Ivan, and Alyosha Karamazov, whose conflicting personalities reflect different views of human nature, faith, reason, and morality. Their corrupt and selfish father, Fyodor Karamazov, becomes the center of a bitter family conflict that culminates in his murder, forcing each brother to confront questions of guilt, freedom, justice, and the existence of God. Through its rich characters and dramatic events, the novel explores some of humanity's deepest dilemmas, ultimately suggesting that love, compassion, and personal responsibility are essential responses to suffering and moral uncertainty.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>My favorite book of all time.</p><h4>Characters</h4><p><strong>Fyodor Pavlovich Karamazov</strong></p><ul><li>Immoral, irreverent, and careless. He is a materialist who is ignorant of all things spiritual or metaphysical.<ul><li>He is also a terrible father, since he neglects his children. <ul><li>He forgets that Sophia is Ivan’s as well as Alyosha’s mother. It’s almost like a “Fruedian slip” in that he likes Alyosha more than Ivan.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Atheistic because he simply doesn’t care. It seems like he has no internal moral sense, and thus, he is not drawn to religion. So, it seems like although he attempts to justify his Atheism with rationality, the reasoning is sound but trivial, and the underlying motivation is simply a lack of a desire to be moral.<ul><li>He criticizes the church for there perceived </li></ul></li><li>On the surface, he seems like a buffoon. In fact, this is how he often refers to himself.<ul><li>However, he is surprisingly rational, though in a petty way. He doesn’t use his rationality for virtuous means. Rather, he uses it to acquire wealth and spend it with little consideration.</li><li>He has this tendency to enjoy diminishing himself, talking down about himself.</li></ul></li><li>Represents the “unconscious”; letting lose repressed emotions in Freudian terms.<ul><li>Is not aware of the existence of the self (or is afraid of the contradictions apparent in his self), and tries to run away from attempting to define his own life through debauchery. </li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Alexandrovich Miusov</strong></p><ul><li>A “man of principle.”<ul><li>Having traveled to the West, he is influenced by Western European ideas of liberalism. In this way, he is also an atheist, except his atheism comes from almost a  feeling of superiority over the people who do believe in faith.</li><li>He does have “principle,” so he does not reject religion on the same basis that Fyodor does. Rather, he rejects it because he finds it superstitious and irrational. Fundamentally, this is grounded upon his liberal values.</li><li>As a result of his principles, he seems to be far more (at least on the surface), open to different ideas. This is seen when he is in the church, where he often expresses his doubts about the institution of religion with internal thoughts, but never expresses them to anyone else.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Ivan Fyodorovich Karamazov</strong></p><ul><li>Intellectual and brilliant. </li><li>In many ways represents a “Greek” way of life.<ul><li>In many ways, he is a Platonist, always looking at the “idea” of something rather than the physical manifestation of that thing.</li><li>Attempts to achieve <em>reinheit</em>, purity in the meaning of “washing oneself clean.” </li><li>Connects with this dislike of being dependent on others. In Ancient Grecian morality, it was viewed as servile and morally degrading to be dependent on another.</li><li>A world of forgiveness is morally repugnant to Ivan. He wants a world of vengeance, and rather the world have “unavenged suffering” than forgiveness. This is a result of his argument against the belief in God outline below.</li><li>Ivan’s rationality is what is preventing him from being able to be a good person. He always thinks to hard about something, which makes it difficult for him to actually do good things.</li></ul></li><li>Often acts as an observer, a bystander, seeking the catalogue and observe the occurrences of the world rather than participate in them.<ul><li>This is also reflected not just in his physical actions, but in the way that he approaches his thinking: for instance, he seems perfectly able to hold contradictory views on the nature of religion and belief, making arguments for each side, yet not believing in any.</li><li>He is able to build moral architecture (his argument for the takeover of the state by the church, which would end crime), despite not having any moral reservations himself. This torments him throughout the book.</li><li>Ivan’s jokes while he talks about the meaning of life and religion is his way of distancing himself (perhaps unconsciously) from these topics.</li><li>He finds it impossible to “love thy neighbor” because he himself is unable to get close to anyone else. He reasons that this is because the “cruelty and evilness of man” prevents him from loving his neighbors.</li></ul></li><li>Independent, and does not want to rely on anybody.<ul><li>He believes that he is above everybody else, so he argues for the takeover of the state by the church, since that would mean that the “weak masses” would have moral guidance. This view is what he espouses through the story of the Grand Inquisitor.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Ivan, “The Brothers Make Friends”</p><p>Very, I want to get to know you once for all, and I want you to know me. And then to say good-bye. <strong>I believe it's always best to get to know people just before leaving them.</strong></p></blockquote><ul><li>Attempts to repress the “base” side of his nature and embrace the “noble” side. It, however, it seems that his base side still shows through at times. <ul><li>When he is describing the torture of children, he says how there is a part of all of us that desire the same sadistic feeling of being able to torture another. What this says is that this desire is also a part of him, and something notable enough that he uses it to make a general claim about the nature of humanity (which may or may not be true).</li><li>His argument for only living until thirty is again another performance of morality, the suppression of his base desire to lengthen his life like Fyodor.</li></ul></li><li>He makes an “anti-mystic” argument against belief in God.<ul><li>Ivan’s argument from “Rebellion”: The “eternal harmony” which is God’s kingdom/heaven is built upon the sum of human history, which includes human suffering. This includes the suffering of children, who have not “eaten the apple” and are thus not responsible for the suffer that is brought upon them. A moral agent cannot accept the suffering of an innocent even if it brings them happiness. To participate in God’s world is to accept this arrangement. Therefore, while God may exist, Ivan chooses to “return the ticket.”<ul><li>Reponses:<ul><li>You are not a spectator that can simply “give back the ticket.” You are already a part of the world, and you cannot simply leave, because you are not a pure, innocent bystander.</li><li>Returning the ticket is not the right response.</li><li>Ivan is a sadist. He is not the innocent bystander. He enjoys watching kids suffer. So he can’t give back the ticket.</li><li>You should try to make the world better, not leave the world.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>It’s not that “God’s motives are beyond human comprehension,” it is this very fact that he rejects. He believes in his human comprehension, and using it, he essentially finds God at fault for the suffering in the world.</li><li>He uses the argument from evil not to disprove the existence of God, but to prove why man ought not to believe in God, even if he does exist.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Alexei Fyodorovich Karamazov</strong></p><ul><li>Intelligent, but not intellectual.</li><li>Seeks purity and transcendence in life, although he attempts this in a vastly different way than Ivan. He is so invested in the spiritual that he seems to disregard the earthly (at least until father Zossima tells him to leave the monastery after his death).<ul><li>He does, however, have a sensual side (he remains a “Karamazov”), which is revealed when he meets Katerina Ivanovna, is and stunned by her beauty.<ul><li>This is again the case after Zossima’s death. As the miracle fails to appear with the body of Zossima stinking, Alyosha loses his faith for some time. </li><li>This is only ended with his realization that love is powerful, as when he showed Grushenka love, she responded by changing herself, and regaining her perspective on life. This leads Alyosha on its own journey, where he reclaims his own faith.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Represents a Christian way of life.<ul><li>Attempts to achieve <em>lauterkeit</em>, purity in the sense of transformation of transfiguration.</li><li>His philosophy of Christian (active) love runs into issue when he loves both side of a conflict, and thus there is no clear way to support one or the other.</li><li>His unconditional love for other is sometimes misinterpreted as being ulterior motives.<ul><li>His early interactions with Lise.</li><li>HIs early interactions with Ilyusha.</li></ul></li><li>Interestingly, he seems to be partially convinced by Ivan during his conversation with him, and abandons some of the principle that are a part of his “Christian love” perspective.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Alyosha in “The Confession of a 'Passionate Heart — in Verse”</p><p>Alyosha was certain that no one in the whole world ever would want to hurt him, and, what is more, he knew that no one could hurt him. This was for him an axiom, assumed once for all without question, and he went his way without hesitation, relying on it.</p></blockquote><ul><li>Dependent on others, but not such that others dislike him for his dependence. This is in line with his Christian morality, a morality that is much more about community and supporting one another.</li><li>He withdraws from the world in order to get away from his sensual side, which seems to afflict every other Karamazov. <ul><li>At the same time, he seems aware of his sensual side, of the potential that he has as a “Karamazov.”</li><li>He is able to provisionally subsume all of his impulses and the need to face up to the contradictions in the self through his love of Zossima. He often hides behind Zossima, in essence hiding behind faith.</li><li>His rejection of his base side is what ultimately leads him to a sense of isolation in the world and his implicit guilt in the murder of his father.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Dimitri Fyodorovich Karamazov</strong></p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Dimitri in “The Confession of a 'Passionate Heart — in Verse”</p><p>The awful thing is that beauty is mysterious as well as terrible. God and the devil are fighting there and the battlefield is the heart of man. But a man always talks of his own ache.</p></blockquote><ul><li>A person of contradictions, attempting to occupy the middle ground between Alexei and Ivan.<ul><li>He is the only character that attempts to occupy the middle, shifting between angel and insect.</li><li>He is the only one who is genuinely trying to reconcile these two sides of himself.</li><li>His contradictions appear in everything that he does, the things that he experiences. He always is able to perceive both the “base” and the “noble” side of anything. <ul><li>“The ice burned my forehead like fire…”</li><li>“Kill oneself from delight…”</li><li>How, despite his hatred of Katerina Ivanovna, he was a “hair-breadth” from “the maddest love.”</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>He is satisfied, even pleased when he heard of Katerina Ivanovna’s humiliation by Grushenka.<ul><li>Shows how he is attracted to the raw sensuality of Grushenka, and not the far more upstanding Katerina.</li></ul></li><li>After he murders Fyodor, he seems to have a moment of realization that matches the moment of realization that occurred to Alyosha.<ul><li>He is no longer as petty as he was before, although he remains emotional. He seems to have gained a larger perspective on life.</li><li>He wins Grushenka back from the Polish officer.</li></ul></li><li>Dimitri suffers for everyone in the world, since he acts is the paragon of what to do when faced with suffering. He accepts it, and digests it, and is able t take control of it. In doing so, he emerges joyful.</li></ul><p><strong>Pavel Fyodorovich Smerdyakov</strong></p><ul><li>Epileptic &amp; intelligent.</li><li>Lacks the contradictions that are present within every other Karamazov (therefore, he is not even referred to as a Karamazov).<ul><li>This could have been snuffed out with the cat-killing incident?</li><li>He has no sense of self that the other members of the Karamazov family do. He doesn’t have a “Karamazov” in him, and thus he is never referred to in that way.</li></ul></li><li>He is like Ivan without a superficial sense of morality.<ul><li>He is a genuine sadist; everything he says is tinged with contempt.</li><li>In fact, Ivan is afraid of him. He scares Ivan during a conversation with him because he truly embodies what Ivan believes in. <ul><li>This exposes Ivan as a sort of Platonist intellectual, since he is afraid, on an instinctual/personal level, of the explication of his ideas.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>He has no personality, no eternal validity.<ul><li>He mirrors Ivan as a result, intellectually and physically.</li><li>When Ivan is not there to support him intellectually, he falls ill.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Lise Khokhlakov</strong></p><ul><li>Masochistic</li><li>She is seems to be pure in the Christian sense.<ul><li>She is an invalid (she suffers), and she is genuine with her emotions (e.g., her love letter to Alyosha).</li><li>This is part of the reason why Alyosha is in love with her.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Katerina Ivanovna Verkhovtseva</strong></p><ul><li>The counterpart of Ivan. </li><li>She invents a reason to say in the “Dimitri drama.”<ul><li>Although she loves Ivan, she plays up a sense of duty towards Dimitri. She enjoys the feeling of moral superiority over others through her self-laceration (it is a “self”-laceration since she choose herself to play this role of the suffering women who <em>must</em> protect her honor before her feelings).</li><li>The primary target of this “self”-laceration is Ivan, since Katerina is essentially inventing moral obligations in order to create moral barriers for him. In this way, she “lacerates” Ivan’s love for her and enjoys it.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Elder Zossima</strong></p><ul><li><s>The most enlightened character in the book.</s><ul><li>He receives this enlightenment stance from his brother, Markel.<ul><li>There is an idea of apostolic succession that is existentialized here, as Markel passes down his “sainthood” to Zossima, who passes it down to Alyosha. <ul><li>Each stage of this changes the form, bringing it closer to the truth.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>The incident where he beats up its subordinate reminds him of the suffering of Markel and the things that he said to him. This changes him and makes him into the person that he would eventually become.</li><li>He joins a monastery, since that is the place where his radical spirituality would be protected.<ul><li>Markel dies because his spirituality was not “protected,” and people just thought he was a crazy person.</li><li>But the retreat to the monastery still faces the problem of “Platonism.”<ul><li>Zossima is not actually in the world, doing something. He is merely relegated to a monastery. And he realizes this, which is why he calls for Alyosha to leave the monastery.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li><li><s>The only character who realizes that the two sides of the soul are equal to one another, that no one should trump another, and they should exist in harmony.</s><ul><li>Becoming a monk is the only means of shedding oneself of superfluous and unnecessary desires.<ul><li>But this does not mean having no desires. It means having the right amount of desire, which is rather Aristotelian.</li></ul></li><li>If this is the case, then why is he a monk? Why does he only seem to pursue the “noble” side of the soul?</li></ul></li><li>He wants to suffer for all of the sins of humanity. He thinks that the monks are the worst sort of people, and that is the reason that they have ended up in the monastery. They are the people who need the most saving.<ul><li>We must always be doing good, and to everyone, since not doing so means “planting a seed of evil” in someone else.</li><li>A moral “butterfly effect” whereby an action has extended consequences, and an evil action plants a seed of evil in someone else, and the infection spread throughout society.<ul><li>Therefore, one is responsible for everything. And, therefore, we must communally confess.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Represents the ideals of the solution presented in the antinomies of the Grand Inquisitor.<ul><li>And this is revealed after his experience of the duel and in his dealings with the mysterious visitor.</li><li>Represents the transformation of the individual through a sort of moral transformation, which is the solution to the contradictions from the Grand Inquisitor chapter.<ul><li>The problem with the Grand Inquisitor is that the people are not transformed like Zossima.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Hell is being unable to love. This refers to the actual Hell, though existentialized and demagicalized.<ul><li>Sin means isolation, and thus confession to no one or just one person (Catholic view) doesn’t help. It requires group confession/confessing to the world/</li><li>This is why he has such a different view of confession than the Catholic Church or the Protestants.</li><li>Confession allows one to release guilt and be able to love others again.</li><li>One is responsible for a lot more suffering than one may realize, even though it may not mean suffering for everyone on Earth.</li><li>He argues for the opposite of Ivan, where the state becomes the church through the process of active love. This is his doctrine of “Christian Socialism.”<ul><li>Moral individualism is the opposite of Christian Socialism; it brings about “destructive\" impotence.” This is exemplified by the onion story.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Mikhail Osipovich Rakitin</strong></p><ul><li>Is the devil incarnate?<ul><li>He tries to tempt Alyosha into after the death of Zossima, in a moment of weakness for him.</li></ul></li><li>Argues against Ivan that immortality is not needed for virtue. Rather, freedom, equality, and fraternity are needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Agrafena Alexandrovna Svetlov</strong></p><ul><li>The personification of sensuality and desire, becoming a point of contention between Dimitri and Fyodor.</li><li>Seems rather like the female double of Fyodor in her willingness to ignore social conventions and flaunt her senuality.</li><li>She is able to be “saved” by Alyosha after his discussion with her and Rakitin.<ul><li>Alyosha makes her understand her own self-worth. He suffers for her sins. He is able to address her respectfully even though she is a wicked women.</li><li>This is the “existentialized miracle”; transformation of a human soul through love.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Captain Nikolai Ilyich Snegiryov and Ilyusha</strong></p><ul><li>A “Greek” family.</li><li>Creates a family that serves as a contrast to the Karamazovs. They are moral, poor, and value honor above all else.<ul><li>Nikolai is ashamed of having folded so quickly in the face of the money that Alyosha offered him, so he rejects it. He is also ashamed of having befriended Alyosha so quickly.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Kolya</strong></p><ul><li>He is at first someone who performs a sort of intellectualism, having been influenced by Ratkitin. <ul><li>He is plagued by blatant contradictions. For instance, though he says that he loves Illusha, he never visits him under the very end.</li></ul></li><li>He is reshaped by Alyosha into someone who is more genuine and loving.</li></ul><p><strong>The Mysterious Visitor</strong></p><ul><li>He wanted to kill Zossima, the only person who knew about his murder. Doing so would allow him to go back to his life as normal.<ul><li>But it would also seal him in hell, as he has not committed two murders, killing his only path out of hell.</li><li>The split second choice not to kill Zossima marked a changed in the person, an indication that they can finally be saved.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>The Grand Inquisitor</h4><ul><li>An antimony between the Roman Catholic Church and Protestantism.<ul><li>But eventually there is going to to be a third way offered.</li><li>Represents the conflict between the base (represented by the GI) and the noble (represented by Jesus).</li></ul></li><li>The Grand Inquisitor argues that what Jesus expects of people is impossible.<ul><li>Thinks that people in general are weak, rebellious, and slavish.</li><li>He gives people the freedom of choice, but does not understand that normal people are simply overwhelmed by this power.</li><li>It may work for the select few, like Jesus, but it won’t work for everybody else.</li><li>And this is where the Catholic Church steps in, as they serve as the earthly agent for the people, taking their freedom of choice away from them (taking on their burden) and making things easier for them.</li><li>The Catholic Church gives people happiness through their authority.</li></ul></li><li>Jesus believes that people, at least deep down, are good and will make the right choice. This is why he offers the choice to people.<ul><li>Thinks that all people are lofty and good.</li></ul></li><li>While Jesus offers total freedom and individuality, the Catholic Church offers total authority and control. Both views seem extreme.</li><li>The three antinomies:<ul><li>Materialism &amp; faith:<ul><li>Jesus argues that you don’t need any food to be moral.<ul><li>The person who exemplifies this is Father Ferapont. </li><li>Ferapont, however, is a completely crazy person. He is presented in a way that makes him arrogant, wild, and prone to hallucinations.</li><li>So this is an argument against Jesus’s position.</li></ul></li><li>The GI argues that you need food in order to be moral.<ul><li>This is the position that is supported in the next section, since in Zossima’s story, he talks about how little the priests survive off of.</li><li>In fact (through the cherry jam examples), it is ok to desire things beyond just the necessities. It becomes only bad when these things become a crutch or an addiction.</li><li>This is similar to the golden mean of Aristotle? You should enjoy things, but do so in a moderate degree.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Community: does faith require others/community?<ul><li>The Protestant view argues that this is not needed.<ul><li>Luther: “Every man is his own pope.”</li><li>A Kierkegaardian view.</li></ul></li><li>The Catholic view is that a central church is needed.<ul><li>There needs to be one universal church so that people will stop killing each other, since they won’t have any more personal religious differences</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Morality/confession:<ul><li>The Lutheran view is that people do not need moral guidance and are free.<ul><li>Suspending the ethical, the individual; can’t be confessed to others since they are uniquely individual.</li></ul></li><li>The Catholic view is that people do, and thus they have created institutions for such guidance.<ul><li>Every person gets moral guidelines by the church that has universal moral guidelines.</li></ul></li><li>The former view means crushing isolation, and the latter means the loss of moral agency.<ul><li>The example given of this is the mysterious stranger, who is totally isolated in his guilt. This guilt is only removed once he confessed to the whole village.</li><li>Hell is not fire and brimstone: it is an existential state of absolute isolation.</li><li>Confession is what allows us to exit this existential isolation, since it brings together the heavenly (the desire to be good) and the earthly (the reality of the crime). This represents the synthesis of the two aspects that have been introduced in the previous sections of the book as a contrast, i.e., the base and the noble, the insect and the Madonna.<ul><li>Confession is not suffering consequences for one’s actions. It is rather a process of alignment, bringing together one’s feels and the way the world is around them.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Miracles:<ul><li>The Lutheran view is that people don’t need miracles.<ul><li>But it is ok to perform miracles if it doesn’t coerce belief.</li></ul></li><li>The Catholic view is that miracles are needed to ensure that people continue to believe in the religion.</li></ul></li><li>Mystery?</li><li>Authority?</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">The Grand Inquisitor</p><p>The most painful secrets of their conscience, all, all they will bring to us, and we shall have an answer for all. And they will be glad to believe our answer, for it will save them from the great anxiety and terrible agony they endure at present in making a free decision for themselves.</p></blockquote><h4>Themes and Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Religion is compatible with science\">Religion is compatible with science</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments\">One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Lying is the root cause of immorality\">Lying is the root cause of immorality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The amount of evil in the world makes the existence of a perfectly loving God unlikely\">The amount of evil in the world makes the existence of a perfectly loving God unlikely</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is easy to love humanity in general, but difficult to love people in particular\">It is easy to love humanity in general, but difficult to love people in particular</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Sincerity means coming to terms with one’s baser side\">Sincerity means coming to terms with one’s baser side</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion\">Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority\">Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering is good, if done correctly\">Suffering is good, if done correctly</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral mastery is achieved through choice\">Moral mastery is achieved through choice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Intellect is a way to rationalize selfishness; impulsiveness is needed to love\">Intellect is a way to rationalize selfishness; impulsiveness is needed to love</a></li><li>Eternal validity: the moral struggles and the identity that makes someone who they are.<ul><li>Smerdyakov lacks this. </li></ul></li><li>Five levels of guilt:<ol><li>The wishing for death (Dimitri)</li><li>Allowing for it to happen, turning a blind eye (Ivan)</li><li>Setting it up, creating the logistical pathway (Ivan)</li><li>Physically telling someone to do it, paying them (Ivan?)</li><li>Committing the act (Serdyukov)</li></ol></li></ul><h4>Meta</h4><ul><li>To give an argument that Dostoevsky want to be convincing, he:<ul><li>Makes the people in the story acknowledge the truth of an argument.</li><li>He puts an argument in the enemy of that view.</li></ul></li><li>Dostoevsky embarks on a journey to demagicalize Christianity since a magical faith coerces belief.<ul><li>At the same time, he is not against miracles. He just thinks that they should be used out of love, not as a demonstration of power.</li></ul></li><li>Dostoevsky utilizes physical sickness as a demonstration of mental sickness:<ul><li>Grushenka gets sick because of her suffering under her spiritual transition with the help of Alyosha.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness\">Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness</a><ul><li>This is similar to the point that is made by the Grand Inquisitor, and the first antimony.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>Ibid. (act in moderation)</li></ul></li></ul>","The EU provides a framework for Libertarian reforms":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Many of the legal and economics frameworks setup in the European Union allow for it to be used in a Libertarian manner.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Currently, the framework is being used to push a left-wing agenda, even though it has the potential for a Libertarian system to be propagated.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>There are laws that ensure an open market and free trade between countries in the EU.</li><li>There are many laws in the EU that ensure individual protections.</li><li>There are many laws in the EU that allow for deregulation to occur.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free trade is generally beneficial for those involved\">Free trade is generally beneficial for those involved</a><ul><li>This is the principle underlying the free trade agreements of countries in the EU.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy\">Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy</a><ul><li>This is the principle underlying protections for individual rights.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a><ul><li>This is the principle underlying the laws that enable deregulation.</li></ul></li></ul>","The difference principle makes false assumptions about people's rationality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The difference principle lies at the heart of Rawls’ justice as fairness. However, it seems that there are some incorrect assumptions made by Rawls in outlining this principle.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This refutes a core component of Rawls’ justice as fairness.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It seems that, behind the veil of ignorance, individuals would not choose the difference principle. Rather, with probabilistic reasoning, they would choose a basic structure which maximizes the average benefit.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a><ul><li>This note is a critique of the difference principle.</li></ul></li></ul>","The effects of capital deepening are uncertain":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Capital deepening is an increase in the ratio of capital to labor (more capital for the same amount of labor), or an increase in labor productivity. This can also be referred to as an increase in capital intensity. It is often considered a necessary but not sufficient factor in economic growth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Capital deepening is important in studying economic growth, as capital deepening is usually seen as important to economic growth.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Depending on the growth model, capital deepening can indefinitely lead to economic growth, or lose it potency due to diminishing returns.</p><ul><li>In the Solow model, the effect of capital deepening will gradually become more muted due to diminishing returns and depreciation.</li><li>In the AK model or some endogenous models, capital deepening can lead to indefinite growth, even without technological progress.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The populace have a right to revolution when the government acts against the General Will":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is a crucial aspect of Rousseau’s theory of the social contract, i.e., the idea that the government signs a contract with the society that it will protect its General Will. So when it fails to do so, the people have a right to rebel.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This idea is naturally derivative of Rousseau’s theory of General Will, and represents an important aspect of his thought.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The General Will represents the general interests of the people in a society, which sets it apart from Locke’s theory of natural rights, since under this view, people rights can be violated in order for the general interest to the protect. However, when the general interest is no longer protected by the government, the people have a right to rebel.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>It seems to be that this theory is much less defensible than Locke’s theory of natural rights, since it is much more difficult to quantify what actions falls under the category of protecting the general interest.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China&#x27;s basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens\">China's basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens</a><ul><li>It seems that China is somewhat following in this model?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge\">Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge</a><ul><li>The consequentialist justification would seems to align well with Rousseau’s view.</li></ul></li></ul>","Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When neither the patient nor the provider is spending the patient’s money, neither party has an interest in the price, so costs rise without any corresponding rise in output or satisfaction.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This locates the defect in the payment structure rather than in any participant’s conduct, which makes it a structural rather than a moral diagnosis of health care costs.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Friedman states the underlying principle bluntly: nobody spends money that is not theirs as carefully as they spend their own. The same structure predicts the growth of administrative cost, since once a large pool of third-party money exists, regulating access to it becomes worthwhile and the regulation is itself an expense.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Insurance companies are not the main reason for the high costs in the US health care system\">Insurance companies are not the main reason for the high costs in the US health care system</a><ul><li>Agrees that the insurer is the wrong target, and locates the cost in providers rather than in the payment structure.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The profit motive sharpens incentives\">The profit motive sharpens incentives</a><ul><li>The general principle of which this is the failure case.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Under standard Medicare, doctors are compensated for the quantity, not quality, of services they provide\">Under standard Medicare, doctors are compensated for the quantity, not quality, of services they provide</a><ul><li>A second incentive defect operating on the provider side.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
